# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings, even when checked out on Windows.
#
# Without this, git's autocrlf rewrites docker/entrypoint.sh to CRLF on a
# Windows clone, the shebang becomes `#!/bin/sh\r`, and the kernel looks for an
# interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r". The container then dies with
#
#   exec /entrypoint.sh: no such file or directory
#
# which names the script, not the interpreter, and sends you looking for a
# missing file that is right there. It works on Linux, so it only ever breaks
# for whoever built the image on Windows.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the files a Linux container reads verbatim.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
docker/** text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# ...and the mirror image: the Windows launchers must KEEP CRLF. cmd.exe is
# unreliable with LF-only batch files (labels and goto in particular), so a
# teammate on Linux normalizing run.bat to LF would break it for everyone on
# Windows — the same bug as above, pointing the other way.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
